findings.claim:
In go-grpc-middleware v2.3.4:
- the stream validator invokes the handler and validates only after each RecvMsg succeeds;
- ignored message types bypass validation;
- the timeout interceptor sets a client deadline but does not stop server-side work;
- real-IP derivation is a configured proxy trust claim, and malformed X-Forwarded-For can stop later headers being tried;
- selector exclusions forward requests directly.

Several renderers lose type or precision:
- CEL legacy formatting shows nested doubles at six decimals, so [0.000000002] displays as [0.000000];
- V2 formatting renders strings and bytes unquoted and sorts by formatted text;
- Format is documented as human-only and unstable;
- Zap JSON retains duplicate keys, while map-based consumers keep one value;
- DeepDiff JSON export can project objects to their properties.

Multiple pinned suites contain tests that pass without checking their stated property:
- a garak encoding test whose parameter list is always empty;
- garak buff tests that are vacuously true on empty input;
- a DeepDiff group whose final expected-state assertion is commented out;
- JWT CI that pipes go test through '|| true';
- garak README claiming 100% coverage while vite.config sets 85% with exclusions;
- a chart test exercising mock-authored clamping.

The framework's evidence-backed claim record has these parts:
- stable ID, owner, reviewer and tenant classification;
- exact predicate and policy authority;
- environment/build identity and the access used;
- positive and negative cases;
- evidence with time, digest, producer and redaction history;
- a status: proposed, attempted, blocked, observed, independently verified, contradicted, stale or revoked;
- exclusions;
- remediation owner, retest and change-based revalidation trigger.
A digest binds bytes but does not prove origin.
